Same "assumes real POSIX kernel facilities" issue as the socket-polling
backend above, but discovered at runtime rather than compile time (see
examples/blam/cblam-testing/WEBRTC_TRANSPORT.md's Phase 3 "first real
browser run" section): BSteamNetworkingSocketsLowLevelAddRef()
unconditionally opens a socketpair() as a self-pipe to wake the
background service thread. Emscripten's libc has no socketpair()
implementation at all -- confirmed at runtime ("warning: unsupported
syscall: __syscall_socketpair"), not just missing a header -- so
GameNetworkingSockets_Init() itself failed outright on every wasm run,
before a single connection was ever attempted. Fixed with a new
IsEmscripten() branch alongside the existing Playstation one ("no
additional setup needed here"): no wake-thread socket is needed at all,
since this project always calls
SteamNetworkingSockets_SetManualPollMode(true) immediately after Init()
on Emscripten (Networking::Networking(), networking.cpp) specifically so
the background service thread never runs there.

That still leaves a narrow window: AddRef() unconditionally tries to
start the service thread *before* the caller's SetManualPollMode(true)
call gets a chance to run (they're two separate statements). Emscripten
pthreads are real Web Workers -- creating one is asynchronous, and
tearing it back down (StopServiceThread()'s join()) blocks the calling
thread until the worker actually starts and exits, which is fatal when
the caller is the browser's real main thread (this project never uses
-sPROXY_TO_PTHREAD, see src/peripheral/platform/CMakeLists.txt). Guarded
both places a service thread can be spun up (AddRef's initial start, and
SetManualPollMode's restart-on-toggle-off branch) with !IsEmscripten()
so it can never happen at all, rather than relying on the two statements
always running back-to-back with no yield point in between.

